# =====================================================================================
# What must never be committed.
#
# This file is a second line of defence, not the first. The first is
# scripts/public-safety-scan.js, which reports the LOCATION and CLASS of anything that
# looks like a credential without ever printing the matched value.
#
# The rule this file encodes: a real credential, a browser profile or a private project
# must never enter the repository, in any branch, at any point in its history. A secret
# that was committed and then deleted is still a secret that was committed - it lives in
# the object database and in every clone and fork.
# =====================================================================================

# ---- credentials -------------------------------------------------------------------
.credentials.yaml
.credentials.yml
credentials.yaml
credentials.json
auth.json
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_rsa.pub
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.pub
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.token
secrets.json

# ---- browser state -----------------------------------------------------------------
# One browser, one profile, one login. None of it belongs in a repository.
profile/
**/profile/
user data/
**/user data/
.playwright/
.playwright-cli/
Cookies
Cookies-journal
Login Data
Login Data-journal
Web Data
Local State
Preferences
Secure Preferences
*.har

# ---- this instance's own state -----------------------------------------------------
.state/
state/
logs/
*.log
*.jsonl
registry/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# The `logs/` and `registry/` entries above are ROOT-ANCHORED, and the bundled harness keeps its own logs
# and project registry UNDER runtime/harness/ - so those two patterns never matched them. The staging tree
# therefore carried runtime/harness/logs/harness.jsonl and runtime/harness/registry/projects.json, both of
# which contain THIS machine's absolute project path, while .gitignore said the case was handled.
# MEASURED, not assumed: the public safety scan reported exactly those two files as MEDIUM
# (machine-specific absolute path) right after a demo project was registered. A registration also writes
# examples/demo-project/.ai/, which the `.ai/` rule elsewhere in this file already covers.
**/logs/
**/registry/

# ---- evidence records --------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence Records are real run output: they contain project paths, prompts, model
# replies and sometimes diffs. They are the product of YOUR work, not source for the
# project, and they are generated on first run.
evidence/
**/evidence/EV-*.json
**/evidence/EV-*.md

# ---- per-project installation state -------------------------------------------------
# A project .ai directory is written when a project is registered with the harness. It holds
# THIS machine absolute project path and that installation task history, and the harness
# scaffolds it again on registration. Shipping one would publish a maintainer path and then
# point a reader project at a directory that does not exist on their machine.
.ai/
**/.ai/

# ---- generated and temporary -------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
# `npm install` writes a lockfile even though this project has NO dependencies, so it appears on a machine that
# has never run npm before and did not exist while the tree was being audited. It is generated, regenerable in
# one command, and carries nothing a reader needs: committing it would add a file that contradicts the
# zero-dependency claim in the README, and leaving it untracked-and-unignored made `git status` dirty and made
# the shipped `tools/check-tree-manifest.js` report the tree as changed on a clone that was perfectly correct.
# MEASURED: a fresh clone plus `npm install` produced an untracked 298-byte package-lock.json and one "added"
# file in the integrity check.
package-lock.json
npm-shrinkwrap.json
tasks.json.tmp-*
*.tmp
*.tmp-*
temp/
.tmp/
tmp/
*.orig
*.rej

# ---- editor and OS -----------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ---- private projects --------------------------------------------------------------
# If you clone this next to a real project you are testing against, keep it out of the
# workbench repository. The scanner cannot guess your project's name; you can.
# /private/
